these are the stars of monkey d luffy  the hero of one piece the trademark one  he has under his left eye that he  inflicted on himself to prove his  determination to become a pirate to  shanks and of course the large gruesome  looking one on his chest in the shape of  a giant x where a kainoos attack hit him  after he had taken ace's life both of  them are reminders of the two most  life-altering moments in luffy's life a  visual representation if you like of  luffy's determination to become a great  pirate and of the tragic loss of his  older brother and his failure at  marinefords and when i thought about it  for a second it hit me it's not only  luffy scars that represent who he is as  a person and what path he follows no  this is true for every single character  in the story that carries some sort of  scar zorro kaido shanks nami  whitebeard every single scar they carry  is a manifestation of what they have  gone through and what they stand for but  it gets even crazier than that because i  think i've discovered that these scars  reveal to us many of the things that  still have to happen before the end of  the story in my opinion making it one of  the most badass ways of storytelling in  the entire story to me and for me luffy  is of course the best person to begin  with because well he's the protagonist  so what do the marks on his rubbery body  tell us about the end of one piece well  as i've said these scars represent the  most important events in luffy's entire  life both of which are still unresolved  the knife cut under his left eye is a  constant reminder throughout the story  that our rubbery boy had one dream and  one dream only his entire life not  becoming a doctor and then a marine and  then a pirate no  luffy wanted to become the pirate king  ever since he met shanks to me it almost  feels like a physical representation of  his oath that he will become a great  pirate and it's also a reminder of  shank's sacrifice the lesson he taught  luffy and the pup with the mountain  bandits and of course the moment that  luffy consumed his devil fruit that  actually was the thing that set him on  his path to become a pirate captain and  start his own crew it also proves to us  and i guess to luffy himself as well  right from the start that he's aware  that the path to freedom will be painful  and full of sacrifices but that he's  still willing to take all of it on and  so i think it's pretty fair to say that  this little scar is a summary of luffy's  origins and his motivation for what he's  doing and trying to achieve throughout  the entire story but what exactly does  this tell us about the ending well as  you've probably guessed this scar also  commemorates his promise to shanks to  return the legendary straw hat that was  passed down from roger to him to luffy  symbolizing shanks's trust in luffy as  the one who can bring the dawn and  change the worlds while the scar keeps  reminding us that luffy is still trying  to become that great pirate it also  promises us a reunion of these two men  that will mark luffy as ready to claim  the throne of the pirate worlds which as  you know i believe should be happening  pretty soon now and so i see this car  basically as a sort of guarantee that we  will have a reunion between shanks and  luffy and of course of luffy becoming  pirate king so that the promise that is  sealed in his cheek now can be fulfilled  shanks by the way who of course carries  two major scars as well his lost arm  though i guess that's a pretty damn big  scar that is basically shanks's  counterpart to luffy's reminding us of  him and their promise meanwhile the  three distinct claw marks over his eyes  that he received during a confrontation  with blackbeard represent his future  quest to stop teach from gaining power  something that he has failed to do so  far this scar also is a promise to us  that the relationship between shanks and  blackbeard will have some sort of payoff  later in the story it could of course be  an actual meeting between these two with  a chance for shanks to fulfill his  mission though we both know that that  probably would have no good ending for  him  or alternatively we will at least get  the story behind shanks's chase of teach  and the secrets that both of them  clearly know about the worlds that we're  still unaware of and so i like to see  this triple scar as a representation of  shanks's all-seeing nature and of his  attempt to stop blackbeard i mean he  even presents it to whitebeard during  their meeting who by the way mentions  his own scars he received from roger  when he sees shanks as well in case  you're wondering why that is it's of  course because shanks was part of  roger's crew so seeing shanks reminds  whitebeard of his old rival and all the  fights and feasts that they've  experienced together really his major  life events and battles commemorated on  his chest they also really nicely  represent whitebeard's character someone  who always stood protectingly in front  of his crew which he called his family  something that he ordered the author of  one piece actually dedicated an entire  page to at his passing showing a very  emotional me at that time that this man  didn't have a single scar on his back  never running away and always facing his  enemies head on without any doubt one of  my all-time favorite moments in the  entire story and it's made extra  powerful because oda sensei doesn't tell  us all this but simply shows us his  scars and the lack thereof on his back  brilliance and of course whitebeard's  death at marineford how he came prepared  to die so that his son ace could live  and how a kind who put a very  unfortunate end to his dreams and era  all of it is deeply engraved in luffy's  chest in the shape of his gaping  x-shaped scar in case you forgot he  received it when his own brother gave  his life to protect his and when jimbei  also risked being killed to protect  luffy as i've discussed in a lot of  detail before in a number of character  analysis i did on luffy marineford and  ace's death were the lowest point in his  arc and the moment that he grew the most  as a character the need to take the  world more seriously to learn that  failure is a real possibility and to get  strong enough to protect his crew and  change the world basically it's another  promise that luffy makes to himself when  he starts his training with rayleigh  that is enclosed in his healing wound in  his chest the x by the way i really like  to think is a nice nod to the x that ace  had in his tattoo in memory of their  third brother sabo's jolly roger and  maybe even more interestingly the scar  also tells us more about luffy and his  ability to make people love and trust  him because it is the product of his  brother and jinbe and the entire  whitebeard pirates as a whole basically  protecting him and once again i think it  does tell us things that will definitely  have to happen in the story there's of  course luffy's eventual encounter with  akainu and how luffy will deal with the  man that killed his brother will he  fight him will he forgive him i really  can't wait to find out and i personally  also like to think that the scar sets up  the possible death of another person  that's close to luffy only that this  time luffy will be able to stop it  whether this will be sabo or one of the  straw hats or someone completely else i  don't know but i feel that this scar  will serve as a reminder to luffy that  he became stronger to not lose another  person like he did with ace i mean if  you wanted to be really poetic about  this you could probably even say that  these scars represent the inherited will  of the people they're connected to  shanks and ace that luffy now carries  and here i thought that scars couldn't  get any cooler but they clearly did okay  but here's the real reason why i think  that these scars let us know exactly  what still needs to happen in the story  because what i've noticed is that  certain fights leave characters like  luffy or zorro with lasting scars well  others don't biggest example here luffy  doesn't have a scar from the fight with  katakuri despite probably being the most  vile wound he received yet or being  impaled by crocodile i mean in theory if  luffy's little knife left a scar  shouldn't he be covered in scars from  all of his fights and so i honestly  think that the reason that only certain  wounds leave scars is because they  represent an unresolved or ongoing  conflict of the character luffy needs to  meet shanks and face a kainu shanks and  blackbeard zaro and mihawk though i do  want to save zorro scars for a whole  separate video if you want to see that  then you of course have the scars of  both akainu and aokiji reminding them of  their unresolved conflict of how the  marines should be led but maybe my  favorite example of all is the scar that  odin gave to kaido the giant cut across  his chest because even though odin is  dead kaido's conflict about that fight  is far from over the fact that he won  this duel in an inhonorable way because  someone distracted odin still haunts him  to this day i go into much more detail  about this all in kaido's character  analysis but to sum it up for here this  scar embodies the burden that kaido has  put on himself to reach his goal to  change the world even if it means going  against his honor that is so important  to him and the joy of fighting against a  strong opponent in a fair fight and so  the scar is there because kaido is still  struggling with what it really  represents  scar across her shoulder embodies her  struggle under arlon and how she  ultimately got saved by luffy and so the  only reason you don't see her scar is  because she decided to put a tattoo over  it making it vanish because the conflict  connected to it has been resolved or  take brille and her scar across her face  that represented her ongoing conflict  with her own family and also category  struggle to be the strong older brother  that can't show any kind of weakness as  you might remember brulee got the scar  after being attacked by other kids until  katakuri stepped in to protect her  katakuri by the way with his own scars  around his mouth that he got from eating  too many donuts pretty cool by the way  that he got bullied for in the end and  thus made him forever hide his face  until luffy came around for him and so  spinning this a bit further i think the  reason why luffy doesn't carry a scar  from this fight with katakuri is because  there is no more conflict between them  they're basically two men who helped  each other and found deep respect for  their opponents now katakuri on the  other hand i wouldn't be at all  surprised if he had kept a scar of where  he stabbed himself after all it's a  really cool reminder of how luffy  changed his life physical proof for his  honor as a man for making the fight fair  and opening up the chance to reunite  with luffy in the future as a possible  ally another fantastic example for this  would be kit and his new scar that he  got from shanks it also embodies the  unresolved conflict between these two  and how kid wants to take down the yonko  above all else and if you want to know  the whole story behind kit and all the  rage and anger that he has and why he  thinks he needs to take down all the  yonko on his own well then you just  really can't miss out on this video  right here where i explain all that and  much much more
 

